SUMMARY:NEATwalks Behind the Stone: Art and Architecture Walk
DESCRIPTION:Neatwalks for newcomers to explore and appreciate Toronto!  \nHighlights   \n\nThe event takes place indoors\, except for a short walk from the subway station\nLearn about the building’s art and architecture\nEnjoy time to take photos and capture memorable moments\n\nSecurity Check  \n\nBe prepared for airport-style security screening \nBring one piece of valid photo ID\, such as a driver’s license\, health card\, or Ontario Photo Card \nPlease avoid bringing large backpacks or bags \n\nRain or Shine  \n\nDress appropriately for the weather \nBring a snack and a bottle of water \nSmall professional cameras are permitted \n\nWho is for : This event is exclusively for adults who are permanent residents or convention refugees. A copy of your immigration documents is required. \nMeeting Point: Inside TTC Queen’s park subway station at 9:15  \nTo register: For new participants please fill this form  \nFor returning participants\, reserve your spot via text (437-249-7103) or email ( rchauhan@culturelink.ca)  \nLove exploring art and architecture? Join us for this unique Toronto experience! 
URL:https://www.culturelink.ca/event/neatwalks-behind-the-stone-art-and-architecture-walk/
LOCATION:Legislative Assembly of Ontario\, 111 Wellesley Street West\, Toronto\, Ontario\, M7A 1A2\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Adults
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://www.culturelink.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Neat-Walks-Web-Icon.jpg
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:CultureLink Settlement Services
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261107T133000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261107T153000
DTSTAMP:20261106T182803Z
CREATED:20261106T180946Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261106T182803Z
UID:10003421-1794058200-1794065400@www.culturelink.ca
SUMMARY:Nature Connection Walk
DESCRIPTION:Explore Ma Moosh Ka Win Valley Trail to Highland Creek Trail (Morningside Park)! \nJoin OLTA\, CultureLink\, and nature poet Sheniz Janmohamed for a guided exploration of the accessible Ma Moosh Ka Win Valley Trail at Morningside Park.  \nReconnect with nature and discover the beauty of the outdoors through poetry\, sensory prompts\, journaling\, and grounding practices. This welcoming experience offers an opportunity to slow down\, engage your senses\, and deepen your connection with the natural world.  \nEveryone is welcome! This event is open to people of all ages\, including children and families. Activities are designed to be inclusive and accessible to people who are learning English.  \nWhat to Bring  \n\nDress appropriately for the weather\nBring a water bottle or thermos\n\nMeet Your Nature Guide  \nSheniz Janmohamed is a certified forest bathing guide\, author\, and land artist. She is passionate about promoting well-being\, fostering meaningful connections with nature\, and encouraging pro-environmental attitudes across communities in the Greater Toronto Area (GTA).  \nJoin us for a refreshing outdoor experience filled with nature\, creativity\, and connection!  \nNearby Landmark – University of Toronto Scarborough (UTSC):\nTo register: Please fill this form  \nFor more information: please contact Rubeen Chauhan at 437-249-7103 or email ( rchauhan@culturelink.ca).
URL:https://www.culturelink.ca/event/nature-connection-walk/
LOCATION:Ma Moosh Ka Win Valley Trail\, 130 Old Kingston Road\, Scarborough\, Ontario\, M1E 3J5\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Families
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://www.culturelink.ca/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/exploring-nature_dareens-SSP.jpg
END:VEVENT
